WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.367 --> 00:00:02.634
-Our first guest tonight
is an Emmy-nominated,

00:00:02.634 --> 00:00:03.934
Golden Globe-winning actress

00:00:03.934 --> 00:00:06.067
you know from shows like
"Felicity" and "The Americans"

00:00:06.067 --> 00:00:08.133
as well as the film
"Cocaine Bear."

00:00:08.133 --> 00:00:09.934
-Whoo!
-Yeah.

00:00:09.934 --> 00:00:11.400
she stars in "The Diplomat,"

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:12.767
which is streaming
now on Netflix.

00:00:12.767 --> 00:00:14.767
Let's take a look.

00:00:14.767 --> 00:00:17.434
-You can wait by that chair,
then you'll be out of eyeshot

00:00:17.434 --> 00:00:20.100
when I open the door
to receive him.

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:21.200
Good.

00:00:23.801 --> 00:00:25.801
-You always have cookies?

00:00:28.033 --> 00:00:29.534
-Is that a problem?

00:00:29.534 --> 00:00:30.634
-For a reprimand?

00:00:30.634 --> 00:00:32.067
-Well, it's hardly
a reprimand, is it?

00:00:32.067 --> 00:00:33.801
-Well, it should look like one.

00:00:33.801 --> 00:00:36.234
-The tell isn't the biscuits.

00:00:36.234 --> 00:00:39.467
It's the American ambassador
lurking in the corner.

00:00:39.467 --> 00:00:41.067
-Please welcome back
to the show

00:00:41.067 --> 00:00:42.400
our friend Keri Russell.

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:43.734
♪♪
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:43.734 --> 00:00:51.300
♪♪

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:58.868
♪♪

00:00:58.868 --> 00:01:01.534
Welcome back.
-Thank you very much.

00:01:01.534 --> 00:01:02.901
Thanks for having me.
-Congratulations on the show.

00:01:02.901 --> 00:01:05.033
I feel like it's a show
everyone's talking about.

00:01:05.033 --> 00:01:06.367
Everyone's talking
about "The Diplomat."

00:01:06.367 --> 00:01:07.501
-Well, that's very nice.

00:01:07.501 --> 00:01:09.767
-For years in "The Americans,"

00:01:09.767 --> 00:01:12.033
you played someone conspiring
against the U.S. government.

00:01:12.033 --> 00:01:16.133
Is it nice to now play a person
within the U.S. government?

00:01:16.133 --> 00:01:17.834
-Well, I figured, you know,
I'm going to continue

00:01:17.834 --> 00:01:20.100
living in the United States.
I better earn my keep.

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:21.400
-Yeah, exactly.

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:23.000
We were all starting to get
a little suspicious

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:24.334
about this Keri Russell
character.

00:01:24.334 --> 00:01:26.234
-You know, you know.
We'll see. We'll see.

00:01:26.234 --> 00:01:28.701
-You obviously shot this
in the U.K. and you shot

00:01:28.701 --> 00:01:30.033
in a lot
of government buildings,

00:01:30.033 --> 00:01:32.567
a lot of places where there were
a lot of protocols.

00:01:32.567 --> 00:01:36.634
-A lot. Generally Americans
and British people

00:01:36.634 --> 00:01:40.200
have different ways of working
in the world generally.

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:44.534
There's a lot of just
more words in a sentence

00:01:44.534 --> 00:01:46.701
there tends to be, you know.
Americans are like,

00:01:46.701 --> 00:01:48.701
"Yes, no," and British people

00:01:48.701 --> 00:01:51.367
are just more polite
about everything.

00:01:51.367 --> 00:01:52.667
And they ask about, you know,

00:01:52.667 --> 00:01:55.067
the weather or your husband
or your pet

00:01:55.067 --> 00:01:56.767
or whatever in this
very lovely way.

00:01:56.767 --> 00:01:57.901
And Americans are like,

00:01:57.901 --> 00:02:00.667
"No, this is what we're doing,
you know?"

00:02:00.667 --> 00:02:02.701
But yes, we shot
in these incredible --

00:02:02.701 --> 00:02:04.701
Like, we shot
at the U.S. embassy,

00:02:04.701 --> 00:02:08.868
which was really incredible,
this state-of-the-art building.

00:02:08.868 --> 00:02:11.200
They actually let us shoot
there, which is incredible.

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:13.133
I'm always like,
It's us and the crew guys.

00:02:13.133 --> 00:02:14.634
Like, they're going to be
eating burritos.

00:02:14.634 --> 00:02:17.234
Like, this is not --
Don't let us in here.

00:02:17.234 --> 00:02:19.801
But they were very gracious,
and these big, beautiful --

00:02:19.801 --> 00:02:23.434
I mean, that's sort of part
of the show is this thing

00:02:23.434 --> 00:02:27.100
that Americans don't exactly
have this this grandeur

00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:29.901
and this, you know,
those stately homes.

00:02:29.901 --> 00:02:32.133
-Yeah. And I think like just
an appreciation

00:02:32.133 --> 00:02:33.567
for the way things
are done a certain way.

00:02:33.567 --> 00:02:35.200
Are you good
with rules like that?

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:37.467
Like, do they tell you before
you go to shoot in a building,

00:02:37.467 --> 00:02:39.300
"Keri, you can't behave
the way you usually do"?

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:40.767
[ Laughter ]

00:02:40.767 --> 00:02:42.033
-I definitely do not invite

00:02:42.033 --> 00:02:44.334
"My mom's gone wild,"
that's for sure.

00:02:44.334 --> 00:02:46.167
-You do have a pretty strong
group of moms gone wild.

00:02:46.167 --> 00:02:48.467
-I have a strong group
of moms gone wild.

00:02:48.467 --> 00:02:51.067
-There have been multiple
complaints in Brooklyn about

00:02:51.067 --> 00:02:53.100
you and your friends.
-I would not be surprised.

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:55.367
I seriously would
not be surprised.

00:02:55.367 --> 00:02:56.734
No, I am a rule follower.

00:02:56.734 --> 00:02:59.434
In fact, on "Felicity,"
way back in the olden days...

00:02:59.434 --> 00:03:00.801
[ Audience cheers ]

00:03:00.801 --> 00:03:05.567
Thanks, guys.
Um, Speedman and Mandy --

00:03:05.567 --> 00:03:06.934
-This is Scott Speedman.
-Scott Speedman.

00:03:06.934 --> 00:03:08.601
-Let's give it up
for my friend Scott Speedman.

00:03:08.601 --> 00:03:10.334
-Yeah, Speedy.

00:03:10.334 --> 00:03:11.901
-Of course. Your brother.
Yes.

00:03:11.901 --> 00:03:14.100
So we would, like, leave
for lunch and do something.

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:15.968
And I was the kid
who was always like, "Guys,

00:03:15.968 --> 00:03:17.667
we have got to get back.

00:03:17.667 --> 00:03:19.968
They are going to be worried and
we have 10 minutes to get back.

00:03:19.968 --> 00:03:21.534
We have must be on time."

00:03:21.534 --> 00:03:23.200
And they used to call me
the homework reminder.

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:24.501
-Really?
-Yeah.

00:03:24.501 --> 00:03:25.968
-And now, you're a
very young person here.

00:03:25.968 --> 00:03:27.968
And I'm assuming "Felicity" is
the first time

00:03:27.968 --> 00:03:30.734
you did a talk show.

00:03:30.734 --> 00:03:32.133
-Yes.

00:03:32.133 --> 00:03:34.133
-Were you --
you were nervous, right?

00:03:34.133 --> 00:03:35.934
-I'm a nervous person.
It's part of me.

00:03:35.934 --> 00:03:37.834
-You do not give off
a nervous vibe.

00:03:37.834 --> 00:03:39.067
-Sweating
uncontrollably right now.

00:03:39.067 --> 00:03:40.167
-Gotcha.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:40.167 --> 00:03:43.367
-So back then,
I was even more nervous

00:03:43.367 --> 00:03:46.834
and I had never done
anything like that.

00:03:46.834 --> 00:03:48.834
And there was no, like, social
media or any of that stuff.

00:03:48.834 --> 00:03:51.100
-There was no preparation either
for being a talk show guest.

00:03:51.100 --> 00:03:52.267
-No.
-Yeah.

00:03:52.267 --> 00:03:54.400
-And it was lovely.

00:03:56.267 --> 00:03:57.367
-Was it Rosie?
-Yes. Yes, thank you very much.

00:03:57.367 --> 00:03:58.601
-It was Rosie,
and you know what else?

00:03:58.601 --> 00:04:00.267
That means it was here.
It was in this studio.

00:04:00.267 --> 00:04:02.400
This is Rosie's old studio.
So if you start having

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:04.267
flashbacks --
-I've wiped it out of my brain.

00:04:04.267 --> 00:04:05.167
[ Laughter ]

00:04:05.167 --> 00:04:07.567
So I was backstage

00:04:07.567 --> 00:04:09.334
and she heard I was nervous.

00:04:09.334 --> 00:04:11.934
And so she very -- she came back
and she said,

00:04:11.934 --> 00:04:14.100
"Hey, you know,
we're going to go do this."

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:16.901
And I was like...
[ Breathing heavily ]

00:04:16.901 --> 00:04:20.567
And I went out and she was being
so polite and so kind to me.

00:04:20.567 --> 00:04:23.167
And you can actually see me.
I'm so nervous.

00:04:23.167 --> 00:04:25.234
And I'm smiling. She's like,
"Where are you from?"

00:04:25.234 --> 00:04:28.734
And I'm like, "Colorado,"
while wiping away a tear,

00:04:28.734 --> 00:04:31.300
like tears coming down my face.
And everyone said,

00:04:31.300 --> 00:04:33.667
"Oh, don't worry, no one will
notice, no one will notice."

00:04:33.667 --> 00:04:35.667
And I went back and watched it.
I'm definitely crying.

00:04:35.667 --> 00:04:37.100
[ Laughter ]
Definitely crying.

00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:39.000
-Well, a lot of people cry
when they get asked,

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:41.901
"Where are you from?"
[ Laughter ]

00:04:41.901 --> 00:04:43.601
-And you know what? It's like...

00:04:43.601 --> 00:04:46.267
It's just part -- I hate
it less about myself.

00:04:46.267 --> 00:04:47.400
And I think that's
what makes it easier.

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:49.834
I used to really
beat myself up about it,

00:04:49.834 --> 00:04:53.501
and it's not normal having
to publicly speak or look --

00:04:53.501 --> 00:04:56.033
-And it's a part of, I think,
acting that people forget.

00:04:56.033 --> 00:04:57.467
And then again,
no one tells you, right?

00:04:57.467 --> 00:04:59.601
You go, you maybe
study it as a craft,

00:04:59.601 --> 00:05:01.033
you get a show
and then you forget

00:05:01.033 --> 00:05:03.267
there's this whole promotional,
ceremonial aspect of it.

00:05:03.267 --> 00:05:05.434
-There is. And they
are two very different --

00:05:05.434 --> 00:05:08.501
Like, someone who's really good
at the ceremonial part

00:05:08.501 --> 00:05:11.234
isn't necessarily great at
acting or, you know what I mean?

00:05:11.234 --> 00:05:13.000
They don't always work together.

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:16.234
So yeah, which is very much
our show and that's...

00:05:16.234 --> 00:05:18.501
-Yeah, you do a lot of ceremony
on "The Diplomat" as well.

00:05:18.501 --> 00:05:20.033
You found a nice fit
for yourself.

00:05:20.033 --> 00:05:21.400
-Yes, I did.

00:05:21.400 --> 00:05:23.834
-You found another
nice fit on "The Americans" --

00:05:23.834 --> 00:05:25.200
your partner, Matthew Rhys.

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:26.834
And I'm a big fan of his,
you know.

00:05:26.834 --> 00:05:28.434
[ Cheers and applause ]
You know.

00:05:28.434 --> 00:05:29.801
You know, I'm a big fan of his.
-Me, too.

00:05:29.801 --> 00:05:32.634
-And the last time he was here,
we had a grand old time.

00:05:32.634 --> 00:05:34.501
I want to make sure.
Was it Saint David's Day?

00:05:34.501 --> 00:05:37.434
-Saint David's Day.
-Which is a big Welsh holiday.

00:05:37.434 --> 00:05:39.067
-It's like the Welsh
St. Patrick's Day.

00:05:39.067 --> 00:05:40.567
-Yes, Welsh St. Patrick's Day.

00:05:40.567 --> 00:05:43.968
And one of the things you do is
there's a leek-eating contest.

00:05:43.968 --> 00:05:45.634
You eat raw leeks.

00:05:45.634 --> 00:05:47.334
And that's what me and --
Man, he's a...

00:05:47.334 --> 00:05:49.934
[ Laughter ]
He's usually a sexy gentleman,

00:05:49.934 --> 00:05:51.434
but when he's chomping
on a leek...

00:05:51.434 --> 00:05:53.667
-He's going for it.
-He's going for it.

00:05:53.667 --> 00:05:56.200
You know, I got the sense
that he was going to,

00:05:56.200 --> 00:05:59.100
on St. David's Day,
maybe tie one on a little bit.

00:05:59.100 --> 00:06:01.400
Like, he had that energy
when he was leaving here.

00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:02.434
Is that your memory of it?

00:06:02.434 --> 00:06:04.701
-We are, you know,
still parents,

00:06:04.701 --> 00:06:07.000
so we're pretty boring
as it goes.

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:08.901
But every once
in a while he does.

00:06:08.901 --> 00:06:13.267
And I will say
he did go out after that even,

00:06:13.267 --> 00:06:16.634
after his time with you
and came home very late

00:06:16.634 --> 00:06:21.634
and in a certain state.
And we actually, in the morning,

00:06:21.634 --> 00:06:23.901
found the footage
of our security,

00:06:23.901 --> 00:06:27.501
like the the security camera of
the front door and rewatched it.

00:06:27.501 --> 00:06:29.434
-You and he together?
-Uh-huh.

00:06:29.434 --> 00:06:31.267
-And was it -- How did he do?

00:06:31.267 --> 00:06:32.868
Do you guys have stairs?

00:06:32.868 --> 00:06:34.968
-We were like,
"Oh, oh, there you are.

00:06:34.968 --> 00:06:37.100
You made it up the stairs.

00:06:37.100 --> 00:06:39.200
Oh, going to find the key.
This might take a while."

00:06:39.200 --> 00:06:42.200
[ Laughter ]
"Oh, he found it. Okay."

00:06:42.200 --> 00:06:45.267
It was absolutely delightful.

00:06:45.267 --> 00:06:47.567
-Obviously you guys
got to collaborate

00:06:47.567 --> 00:06:50.868
on "The Americans."
You were both in --

00:06:50.868 --> 00:06:53.100
First of all, congratulations on
"Cocaine Bear."

00:06:53.100 --> 00:06:54.634
I mean, for real?
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:54.634 --> 00:06:56.734
-Whoa! Thanks.

00:06:56.734 --> 00:06:58.567
-It was such an exciting thing.

00:06:58.567 --> 00:07:01.167
-How crazy? So crazy.
-But it was an --

00:07:01.167 --> 00:07:02.601
When you heard about it,
you were like,

00:07:02.601 --> 00:07:04.634
"Oh, this is going to be fun."
And then it was deeply fun.

00:07:04.634 --> 00:07:06.801
People were very excited
to see it.

00:07:06.801 --> 00:07:08.434
Matthew was in "Cocaine Bear."
-He was.

00:07:08.434 --> 00:07:09.968
-He did not last very long.

00:07:09.968 --> 00:07:11.868
No spoiler -- That's a spoiler.
-No spoilers.

00:07:11.868 --> 00:07:14.133
-But if you go to see
a lot of Matthew,

00:07:14.133 --> 00:07:15.467
you're going to see a little.
-A little.

00:07:15.467 --> 00:07:16.767
-Yeah.
-But he's going to go out

00:07:16.767 --> 00:07:18.734
like a firecracker.
-He did. Yeah.

00:07:18.734 --> 00:07:21.534
He made his time in "Cocaine
Bear" deeply worth it. Yeah.

00:07:21.534 --> 00:07:23.567
-Stamp. Yeah.

00:07:23.567 --> 00:07:25.501
-Now, have you stayed
in touch with the bear?

00:07:25.501 --> 00:07:27.767
Yes. Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:27.767 --> 00:07:30.834
So when you shoot scenes
like this crazy movie...

00:07:30.834 --> 00:07:32.167
-Yeah, this is a very --

00:07:32.167 --> 00:07:33.567
I mean, this is all you
need for "Cocaine Bear."

00:07:33.567 --> 00:07:34.968
-That's the movie.

00:07:34.968 --> 00:07:38.734
-As a still for a film goes,
that's great.

00:07:38.734 --> 00:07:41.334
-The suit. The hot pink suit
and the handbag.

00:07:41.334 --> 00:07:42.667
Okay. Yeah.

00:07:42.667 --> 00:07:44.801
So when you shoot those, like,
bear attacking scenes,

00:07:44.801 --> 00:07:48.501
it's actually this really
lovely, you know,

00:07:48.501 --> 00:07:51.701
Kiwi, New Zealand guy,
large man,

00:07:51.701 --> 00:07:55.334
muscular, like, whatever,
dressed in a skintight unitard.

00:07:55.334 --> 00:07:57.634
-Yeah.
-Crawling around
looking like a bear.

00:07:57.634 --> 00:07:58.801
-Yeah.

00:07:58.801 --> 00:08:02.801
-So maybe not always scary.
-Right.

00:08:02.801 --> 00:08:06.334
-But strange. And then -- So
it's either him, lovely Allan,

00:08:06.334 --> 00:08:09.200
climb-- you know,
doing his amazing movements

00:08:09.200 --> 00:08:11.667
and you're kind of
watching or -- Banks

00:08:11.667 --> 00:08:13.434
Elizabeth Banks yelling.
-Yeah.

00:08:13.434 --> 00:08:16.267
-So that's like --
So I guess that's -- Yeah.

00:08:16.267 --> 00:08:18.701
See now, that looks --
If I saw that in the woods,

00:08:18.701 --> 00:08:19.901
that's scarier to me
than a bear.

00:08:19.901 --> 00:08:22.968
-It is scary.
That is slightly scary.

00:08:22.968 --> 00:08:25.567
But then otherwise it's Banks
yelling to you like,

00:08:25.567 --> 00:08:27.801
"Oh, my gosh!"
You know, with a megaphone.

00:08:27.801 --> 00:08:30.300
"His arm is being eaten off!
His leg's throwing!

00:08:30.300 --> 00:08:33.133
There's blood everywhere! His
face is dripping with blood!"

00:08:33.133 --> 00:08:34.434
And that's what
you're reacting to.

00:08:34.434 --> 00:08:36.267
-I do have a third photo,

00:08:36.267 --> 00:08:38.701
which is, I think, the scariest
of the options to see.

00:08:38.701 --> 00:08:41.868
We have guy in bear suit.
We have weird half a bear

00:08:41.868 --> 00:08:43.868
and then we just have
a solitary bear head.

00:08:43.868 --> 00:08:45.567
Oh.
[ Laughter ]

00:08:45.567 --> 00:08:47.667
There it is.
-This is what you're looking at.

00:08:47.667 --> 00:08:50.434
You're like, "Oh, got it.

00:08:50.434 --> 00:08:52.100
Scary.
It's very, very scary."

00:08:52.100 --> 00:08:54.767
-Did you have -- I mean,
you're making the movie.

00:08:54.767 --> 00:08:56.801
Did you have any sense
of the cultural impact

00:08:56.801 --> 00:08:59.467
that it was going to have?
No, I genuinely mean,

00:08:59.467 --> 00:09:02.367
like, you know, it's so hard
these days for a movie

00:09:02.367 --> 00:09:04.367
to happen
where it's not based on,

00:09:04.367 --> 00:09:07.467
you know, a Marvel film
or a Star Wars film

00:09:07.467 --> 00:09:09.567
or some sort of, you know,
intellectual property.

00:09:09.567 --> 00:09:11.901
And you guys were just like,
"Cocaine bear, let's go."

00:09:11.901 --> 00:09:15.300
-We're going to go all in for
"Cocaine Bear." I love it.

00:09:15.300 --> 00:09:17.868
The pandemic got weird.
What can I say?

00:09:17.868 --> 00:09:19.834
-Yeah. People were like,
"Do we need another --

00:09:19.834 --> 00:09:21.367
We should do another
Avengers movie."

00:09:21.367 --> 00:09:24.067
And you're like, "Or should we
have a bear eat some cocaine?"

00:09:24.067 --> 00:09:26.467
[ Laughter ]

00:09:26.467 --> 00:09:27.601
-I guess I bet right.

00:09:27.601 --> 00:09:30.334
-It was really great.
You did bet right.

00:09:30.334 --> 00:09:31.634
I mean, Elizabeth Banks
did such a fantastic job.

00:09:31.634 --> 00:09:35.100
-She did such a fantastic --
I'm so happy for her.

00:09:35.100 --> 00:09:37.767
I mean, this is --
She's so awesome anyway,

00:09:37.767 --> 00:09:40.467
but this is huge for her.
This is so great.

00:09:40.467 --> 00:09:42.634
-That's great.
The new show is great.

00:09:42.634 --> 00:09:43.868
And you as always are great.

00:09:43.868 --> 00:09:44.968
Thanks so much
